<section class="w-full max-w-page mx-auto px-6 mt-section" aria-labelledby="br-confirmed">
    <h2 id="br-confirmed" class="cs-heading">What is confirmed</h2>
    <ul class="br-evidence">
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">Dell built Sales Chat for its sellers, "rolled it out to all of our sellers and turned off all the other systems". Sellers use it to "access information, distill it, create content, even automate emails."</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source">John Roese, Dell Global CTO and Chief AI Officer. <a href="https://www.arnnet.com.au/article/4048743/the-first-step-to-ai-is-to-become-customer-zero-says-dell-global-cto.html">ARN, 1 Sep 2025</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">It is "the only system that must remain online" for Dell's sales teams. The systems feeding it "can temporarily fail".</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source">Roese. <a href="https://www.dqindia.com/news/dells-five-tech-predictions-for-2026-ai-at-scale-agents-sovereign-clouds-and-the-future-enterprise-10902294">DQ India, Dec 2025</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">It saves sellers "three to five hours a week" by "curating content from across a distributed data estate".</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source"><a href="https://www.dell.com/en-us/blog/how-dell-storage-powers-ai-factories/">Dell blog, 11 Mar 2026</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">Dell's CFO calls it "our own internal sales chat CRM model", which has "handed back multiple hours per week" to sellers.</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source"><a href="https://fortune.com/2026/03/30/dells-cfo-is-using-ai-agents-to-run-his-finance-team-and-has-helped-the-ai-business-go-from-0-to-25-billion/">Fortune, 30 Mar 2026</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">Sales is one of the few areas Dell picked for AI under its "Customer Zero" programme, alongside supply chain, services and engineering. Dell cut about 800 AI ideas down to a handful.</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source"><a href="https://fortune.com/2025/01/22/dell-cto-gen-ai-roi/">Fortune, 22 Jan 2025</a>; <a href="https://siliconangle.com/2026/05/12/dell-technologies-world-ai-factory-thecube-delltechworld/">SiliconANGLE, 12 May 2026</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">Dell's new partner platform is an "agentic partner experience" (AI that takes actions, not just answers questions), "powered by a family of AI assistants". It covers demand signals, deal registration and real-time pricing, and rolls out in the second half of 2026.</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source">Dell Technologies World, 18 May 2026. <a href="https://channelbuzz.ca/2026/05/the-buzz-dell-technologies-unveils-ai-powered-partner-platform-and-expands-the-ai-factory-46628/">Channel Buzz</a>; <a href="https://futurumgroup.com/insights/dells-new-partner-program-blueprint-for-the-ai-era/">Futurum</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">Dell's headcount is about 97,000, down roughly 11,000 in a year. The stated reasons are reorganisation and limits on external hiring.</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source"><a href="https://investors.delltechnologies.com/static-files/2150fa65-e520-4a31-bf12-81d32e16670f">Dell FY2026 10-K, Mar 2026</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="br-evidence__item">
            <p class="br-evidence__claim">Dell's CMO calls becoming AI-native "existential" and says buyers now start their journeys with AI tools.</p>
            <p class="br-evidence__source"><a href="https://siliconangle.com/2026/05/28/becoming-an-ai-native-company-delltechworld/">SiliconANGLE, 28 May 2026</a></p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="w-full max-w-page mx-auto px-6 mt-section" aria-labelledby="br-gaps">
    <h2 id="br-gaps" class="cs-heading">What we can't confirm yet</h2>
    <div class="br-boxes">
        <div class="br-box">
            <h3 class="br-box__title">Claimed, but unverified <span class="br-tag br-tag--unverified">Unverified</span></h3>
            <ul class="cs-list">
                <li>The hours-saved figures don't agree: 3 to 5 a week on Dell's blog, 13 in TIME (Sep 2026), which doesn't name the tool. Treat both as Dell's own claims.</li>
                <li>Roese reportedly said Dell had to clean up its sales data before launch, and that the tool searches Dell's documents by meaning. Podcast page blocked; search summary only.</li>
                <li>Dell's 2025 analyst meeting reportedly called it "the most modern sales tool we've ever given our sales force". Transcript blocked; search summary only.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="br-box">
            <h3 class="br-box__title">Not known</h3>
            <ul class="cs-list">
                <li>Which vendor or AI models it runs on. Dell uses Glean internally, but nobody links Glean to Sales Chat.</li>
                <li>Exactly what content it pulls from, and who at Dell owns that content.</li>
                <li>Whether it handles pricing, specs, proposals or other languages.</li>
                <li>Whether partners will get it or something like it.</li>
                <li>Its launch date.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="w-full max-w-page mx-auto px-6 mt-section" aria-labelledby="br-fit">
    <h2 id="br-fit" class="cs-heading">Where it's strong, and where it isn't</h2>
    <div class="cs-cards">
        <div class="cs-card">
            <span class="cs-card__number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
            <h3 class="cs-card__title">Good at</h3>
            <div class="cs-card__body"><ul class="cs-list">
                <li>Finding and summarising existing content</li>
                <li>First drafts</li>
                <li>Call prep and cutting admin</li>
            </ul></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-card">
            <span class="cs-card__number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
            <h3 class="cs-card__title">Falls short</h3>
            <div class="cs-card__body"><ul class="cs-list">
                <li>Repeats stale or wrong source content confidently</li>
                <li>Tone, idiom and local relevance in other languages</li>
                <li>Buyer trust: most buyers still want a person to check</li>
            </ul></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-card">
            <span class="cs-card__number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
            <h3 class="cs-card__title">Can't do</h3>
            <div class="cs-card__body"><ul class="cs-list">
                <li>Live events and roundtables</li>
                <li>Filmed video with real people</li>
                <li>Sign-off across several companies</li>
                <li>New research or customer proof</li>
            </ul></div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="br-note"><span class="br-tag br-tag--view">Our view</span> Based on independent research into AI sales tools in general (Gartner, May 2026; Appen; an ACL 2026 benchmark where the best model scored 2.1 out of 3 on cultural adaptation), not on Sales Chat specifically.</p>
</section>

<section class="w-full max-w-page mx-auto px-6 mt-section" aria-labelledby="br-objections">
    <h2 id="br-objections" class="cs-heading">Fair objections</h2>
    <div class="br-faq">
        <details>
            <summary>Is Sales Chat really what's eating our work?</summary>
            <p>We can't prove it from public sources. What we can say is that Dell has deliberately made one AI tool the place sellers go for content, and it lets them draft their own. Routine enablement copy is the work most likely to be affected. It's worth checking our own job history against when Sales Chat launched.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>Isn't this just AI hype?</summary>
            <p>Some of it is. Dell's hours-saved numbers are self-reported and contradict each other. But the structural facts don't depend on those numbers: the tool is live, it's mandatory, and the old tools are gone.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>Dell will just do it all in-house.</summary>
            <p>Possibly, and the headcount cuts point that way. But there's no public sign of an agency review. Dell's CMO also says Dell uses partners to fill gaps in its internal readiness. The risk is real, but it hasn't happened.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
            <summary>We don't know enough to act.</summary>
            <p>Agreed, which is why the first step below is about finding out, not committing.</p>
        </details>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="w-full max-w-page mx-auto px-6 mt-section" aria-labelledby="br-next">
    <div class="cs-role">
        <h2 id="br-next" class="cs-role__title">Next steps</h2>
        <ul class="cs-list">
            <li><strong>Find out who owns Sales Chat's source content.</strong> Ask our Dell contacts which team runs it, what content it pulls from, and whether any One1000-made content is in there. That tells us if our work is feeding the tool or being bypassed by it.</li>
            <li><strong>Check our own job history.</strong> Have battlecards, one-pagers, talk tracks or seller emails dropped off since mid-2025? That shows how big the problem actually is.</li>
            <li><strong>Then commit to the plan below.</strong> The first two steps tell us how hard to push on each part.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="w-full max-w-page mx-auto px-6 mt-section" aria-labelledby="br-options">
    <h2 id="br-options" class="cs-heading">The plan <span class="br-tag br-tag--view">Our view</span></h2>
    <div class="br-boxes">
        <div class="br-box">
            <h3 class="br-box__title">Short to medium term: own the cutting-edge AI events</h3>
            <ul class="cs-list">
                <li>Pitch and run hackathons, hands-on AI labs and expert-led sessions. That's where Dell and NVIDIA most want their customers' attention right now.</li>
                <li>Build on the Dell x NVIDIA lab we're already running in EMEA, and bring in the specialists these formats need.</li>
                <li>Much of this content currently comes out of Dell's San Francisco offices, with a few events in London. Running more of it here, and running it well, is the opening.</li>
                <li>Every event produces the digital content around it: films, recaps, follow-up kits. That's our moat, and Sales Chat can't host a room.</li>
                <li>Pitch more complex content that AI can't produce in one go, such as e-guides. AI can already draft long copy. What it can't do is the work around it: interviewing experts, shaping the argument, design, legal and product sign-off, and adapting it for each market. Sell that, not word count, and the work stays defensible for the next few years as the models improve.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="br-box">
            <h3 class="br-box__title">Longer term: de-risk by winning clients beyond Dell</h3>
            <ul class="cs-list">
                <li>Relying on one client is a bigger risk than any single tool. Sales Chat just makes it visible.</li>
                <li>The AI-event track record above is exactly what other tech brands will want to see, so the two halves of the plan feed each other.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    <h3 class="br-box__title mt-content">Supporting options, if the first steps point that way</h3>
    <ol class="br-points">
        <li class="br-point"><div>
            <h3 class="br-point__title">Keep Sales Chat's source content in shape</h3>
            <p class="br-point__body">We write and maintain the approved content the tool draws on, retire stale assets, and report gaps based on what sellers ask. This works with the tool rather than against it.</p>
        </div></li>
        <li class="br-point"><div>
            <h3 class="br-point__title">Check AI output for brand and legal compliance</h3>
            <p class="br-point__body">We already do brand and legal compliance work for Dell. Forrester predicts a Fortune 500 firm will sue a B2B supplier over AI-generated misrepresentation in 2026.</p>
        </div></li>
        <li class="br-point"><div>
            <h3 class="br-point__title">Prepare for the partner platform</h3>
            <p class="br-point__body">Templates, tone guidance and approval rules for content the partner AI assembles.</p>
        </div></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="br-note">Worth repricing over time: routine one-pagers, battlecard refreshes, straight translation and hours-based production. Procurement teams across the industry now expect AI savings on this kind of work (<a class="underline" href="https://digiday.com/marketing/as-ai-alters-cost-of-creative-indie-agencies-review-how-they-charge-clients/">Digiday, 8 Aug 2025</a>).</p>
</section>

